be a continuous map of topological spaces.\nConsider the sheaves of continuous real valued functions\n<MATH_0002> on <MATH_0003> and <MATH_0004> on <MATH_0005>,\nsee Example <REF_0001>. We claim that there\nis a natural <MATH_0006>-map <MATH_0007>\nassociated to <MATH_0008>. Namely, we simply define it by the\nrule\n<MATH_0009>\nStrictly speaking we should write <MATH_0010>.\nIt is clear that this is a family of maps as in\nDefinition <REF_0002> and compatible with the <MATH_0011>-algebra\nstructures. Hence it is an <MATH_0012>-map of sheaves of <MATH_0013>-algebras, see\nLemma <REF_0003>.\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\nOf course there are lots of other situations where there is\na canonical morphism of ringed spaces associated to a geometrical\ntype of morphism. For example, if <MATH_0014>, <MATH_0015> are <MATH_0016>-manifolds\nand <MATH_0017> is an infinitely differentiable map, then\n<MATH_0018> induces a canonical morphism of ringed spaces\n<MATH_0019>.\nThe construction (which is identical to the above) is left\nto the reader.\n<STRUCT_0004>", "source_text_hash": "sha256:174d610cb687bee2ab2505a449caf15d60190810b7d96c2abaece07702ad34f5", "unit_id": "inventory:sheaves:0092:example/0001"}}}, {"context_id": "sha256:74775e08ba64725421ecabca4571716ad7286c1e57e6c0f18ff87d19288701f4", "hash": "sha256:74775e08ba64725421ecabca4571716ad7286c1e57e6c0f18ff87d19288701f4", "value": {"kind": "locked-English-reference", "location": {"byte_end": 35595, "byte_start": 34571, "file": "sheaves.tex", "fragment_hash": "sha256:bfad5770a8e69f1914188385b64e8dc2afbc7ac03c0c8d8c14840c837a59dcd7", "line_end": 1030, "line_start": 1009}, "owner_tag": "007C", "parent_tag": "0078", "requested_label": "sheaves-example-germs-functions", "requested_tag": "007C", "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_unit": {"chapter": "sheaves", "node_kind": "example", "parent_tag": "0078", "placeholders": {"LOCKED_0001": "\\label{example-germs-functions}", "MATH_0001": "$X = \\math